    "body": "<p>Soundscape restoration asks a simple question: what does a place sound like when it is allowed to heal? Protected areas, rewilded land, and restored wetlands show the answer acoustically: as vegetation returns and disturbance falls, bird song, frog choruses, and insect bands return, and the soundscape&#x27;s richness rises measurably. The quiet-places movement takes the same idea further, seeking to protect naturally quiet places from noise intrusion. Gordon Hempton, an acoustic ecologist, identified One Square Inch of Silence in Olympic National Park in Washington State as a place to protect from noise, arguing that a single quiet square inch, kept free of human noise, symbolises the protection of natural quiet everywhere. Quiet is not the goal for its own sake: the goal is a soundscape in which biophony and geophony can be heard clearly, with anthrophony present but not dominant. This record covers all three layers, because restoration is judged by their balance.</p><h4>Science proof</h4><p>Science proof: restoration acoustics studies compare restored and reference sites and find that acoustic richness and biophony levels recover alongside vegetation and animal communities, making sound a rapid restoration indicator. The One Square Inch of Silence project is a documented conservation effort with published reasoning about natural quiet. Soundscape-perception research supports the restorative value of natural sound for human listeners as well as its ecological value.</p><h4>Math proof</h4><p>Math proof: if a restored site&#x27;s biophony power rises from 0.4 to 0.8 while anthrophony power stays at 0.2, NDSI rises from (0.4 - 0.2) / (0.4 + 0.2) = 0.33 to (0.8 - 0.2) / (0.8 + 0.2) = 0.60. The demo prints NDSI 0.33 before restoration and 0.60 after, plus the improvement, 0.60 - 0.33 = 0.27.</p><h4>Soundscape layer(s)</h4><p>biophony; geophony; anthrophony</p><h4>Source</h4><p>Published restoration acoustics literature; Gordon Hempton, One Square Inch of Silence project documentation, as published October 2026</p>",
